#: ../../../prompt.rst:300
msgid "Useful imports"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:302
msgid ""
"There are a few useful ``prompt_toolkit`` tools that will help us create "
"better bindings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:305
msgid ""
"from prompt_toolkit.keys import Keys\n"
"from prompt_toolkit.filters import Condition, EmacsInsertMode, ViInsertMode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:311
msgid "Custom keyload function"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:313
msgid ""
"We need our additional keybindings to load after the shell is initialized, "
"so we define a function that contains all of the custom keybindings and "
"decorate it with the appropriate event, in this case ``on_ptk_create``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:317
msgid ""
"We'll start with a toy example that just inserts the text \"hi\" into the "
"current line of the prompt:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:319
msgid ""
"@events.on_ptk_create\n"
"def custom_keybindings(bindings, **kw):\n"
"\n"
"    @bindings.add(Keys.ControlW)  # or just \"c-w\" string\n"
"    def say_hi(event):\n"
"        event.current_buffer.insert_text('hi')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:328
msgid ""
"Put that in your :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>`, restart xonsh and then see if "
"pressing ``Ctrl-w`` does anything (it should!)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:332
msgid "What commands can keybindings run?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:334
msgid ""
"Pretty much anything! Since we're defining these commands after xonsh has "
"started up, we can create keybinding events that run subprocess commands "
"with hardly any effort at all. If we wanted to, say, have a command that "
"runs ``ls -l`` in the current directory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:339
msgid ""
"from prompt_toolkit.application import run_in_terminal\n"
"\n"
"@events.on_ptk_create\n"
"def custom_keybindings(bindings, **kw):\n"
"\n"
"    @bindings.add(Keys.ControlP)\n"
"    def run_ls(event):\n"
"        def _task():\n"
"            ls -l\n"
"        run_in_terminal(_task)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:353
msgid ""
"``run_in_terminal(func)`` (imported from ``prompt_toolkit.application``) is "
"the canonical ``prompt_toolkit`` idiom for running code that writes to "
"``STDOUT`` from a keybinding. It temporarily hides the prompt, runs your "
"function (which can freely ``print`` or launch subprocesses), then redraws "
"the prompt — including ``$RIGHT_PROMPT`` and ``$BOTTOM_TOOLBAR`` — above the "
"captured output. Do **not** use ``event.cli.renderer.erase()`` for this "
"purpose: it resets the renderer's height bookkeeping and ``$BOTTOM_TOOLBAR`` "
"(and sometimes ``$RIGHT_PROMPT``) will disappear until the next keypress."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:364
msgid "Restrict actions with filters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:366
msgid ""
"Often we want a key command to only work if certain conditions are met. For "
"instance, the ``<TAB>`` key in xonsh brings up the completions menu, but "
"then it also cycles through the available completions. We use filters to "
"create this behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:371
msgid ""
"A few helpful filters are included with ``prompt_toolkit``, like "
"``ViInsertMode`` and ``EmacsInsertMode``, which return ``True`` when the "
"respective insert mode is active."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:375
msgid ""
"But it's also easy to create our own filters that take advantage of xonsh's "
"beautiful strangeness. Suppose we want a filter to restrict a given command "
"to run only when there are fewer than ten files in a given directory. We "
"just need a function that returns a Bool that matches that requirement and "
"then we decorate it! And remember, those functions can be in xonsh-language, "
"not just pure Python:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:379
msgid ""
"from prompt_toolkit.filters import Condition\n"
"\n"
"@Condition\n"
"def lt_ten_files():\n"
"    return len(g`*`) < 10"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:387
msgid ""
"See `the tutorial section on globbing <tutorial.html#normal-globbing>`_ for "
"more globbing options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:390
msgid ""
"Now that the condition is defined, we can pass it as a ``filter`` keyword to "
"a keybinding definition:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:392
msgid ""
"@events.on_ptk_create\n"
"def custom_keybindings(bindings, **kw):\n"
"\n"
"    @bindings.add(Keys.ControlL, filter=lt_ten_files)\n"
"    def ls_if_lt_ten(event):\n"
"        def _task():\n"
"            ls -l\n"
"        run_in_terminal(_task)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:403
msgid ""
"With both of those in your :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>`, pressing ``Control L`` "
"will list the contents of your current directory if there are fewer than 10 "
"items in it. Useful? Debatable. Powerful? Yes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:408
msgid "Edit-mode filter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:410
msgid ""
"A common use of filters is restricting a binding to a specific editing mode "
"— Emacs insert, Vi insert, or Vi navigation. ``prompt_toolkit`` ships ready-"
"made filters for each; pass them as ``filter`` to ``bindings.add``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:415
msgid ""
"from prompt_toolkit.filters import (\n"
"    EmacsInsertMode, ViInsertMode, ViNavigationMode,\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"@events.on_ptk_create\n"
"def custom_keybindings(bindings, **kw):\n"
"\n"
"    @bindings.add('c-l', filter=EmacsInsertMode())\n"
"    def ls_in_emacs_insert(event):\n"
"        def _task():\n"
"            ls -l\n"
"        run_in_terminal(_task)\n"
"\n"
"    @bindings.add('c-l', filter=ViInsertMode() | ViNavigationMode())\n"
"    def ls_in_any_vi_mode(event):\n"
"        def _task():\n"
"            ls -l\n"
"        run_in_terminal(_task)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:436
msgid ""
"The same key (``Ctrl-L``) can be bound to different actions per mode — "
"``prompt_toolkit`` picks the binding whose ``filter`` is currently true. "
"Combine with custom ``Condition`` filters using ``&``, ``|``, ``~`` (as in "
"``ViInsertMode() & lt_ten_files``) for finer-grained rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:443
msgid "Pre-filling the next command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:445
msgid ""
"xonsh can pre-fill the prompt input for the next command using two "
"environment variables. This is useful for building interactive workflows, "
"wizards, or keybindings that prepare a command for the user to review and "
"edit before running."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:450
msgid "``$XONSH_PROMPT_NEXT_CMD``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:452
msgid "Sets the text that will appear in the next prompt as editable input:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:454
msgid "$XONSH_PROMPT_NEXT_CMD = 'git commit -m \"\"'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:458
msgid ""
"The next time the prompt appears, ``git commit -m \"\"`` will be pre-filled "
"and the user can edit it before pressing Enter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:461
msgid ""
"**Cursor positioning:** Use the ``<cursor>`` marker to place the cursor at a "
"specific position:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:464
msgid "$XONSH_PROMPT_NEXT_CMD = 'git commit -m \"<cursor>\"'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:468
msgid ""
"The marker is removed from the text and the cursor is placed at its position "
"— in this case, between the quotes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:471
msgid "**Example — a keybinding that prepares a git commit:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:473
msgid ""
"@events.on_ptk_create\n"
"def custom_keybindings(bindings, **kw):\n"
"\n"
"    @bindings.add('c-g')\n"
"    def prepare_commit(event):\n"
"        $XONSH_PROMPT_NEXT_CMD = 'git commit -m \"<cursor>\"'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:482
msgid ""
"Now pressing ``Ctrl-G`` will pre-fill the next prompt with ``git commit -m "
"\"\"`` and place the cursor between the quotes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:486
msgid "``$XONSH_PROMPT_NEXT_CMD_SUGGESTION``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:488
msgid ""
"Sets a greyed-out suggestion (like auto-suggest from history) for the next "
"prompt. The user can accept it by pressing the right arrow key:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:491
msgid "@ $XONSH_PROMPT_NEXT_CMD_SUGGESTION = 'git push origin main'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:495
msgid ""
"Unlike ``$XONSH_PROMPT_NEXT_CMD``, this does not pre-fill the input — it "
"only shows a suggestion that the user can accept or ignore."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:498
msgid ""
"Both variables are cleared automatically after being consumed by the prompt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:502
msgid "Virtual Environment in Prompt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:504
msgid ""
"xonsh obeys the ``$VIRTUAL_ENV_DISABLE_PROMPT`` environment variable `as "
"defined by virtualenv <https://virtualenv.pypa.io/en/latest/how-to/usage."
"html#customize-prompt>`__. If this variable is truthy, xonsh will *always* "
"substitute an empty string for ``{env_name}``. Note that unlike other "
"shells, ``$VIRTUAL_ENV_DISABLE_PROMPT`` takes effect *immediately* after "
"being set --- it is not necessary to re-activate the environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:511
msgid ""
"xonsh also allows for an explicit override of the rendering of ``{env_name}"
"``, via the ``$VIRTUAL_ENV_PROMPT`` environment variable. If this variable "
"is set to a non-empty value, ``{env_name}`` will *always* render as its "
"value, regardless of whether a virtual environment is active. The value is "
"used as-is, without the usual ``{env_prefix}`` / ``{env_postfix}`` wrapping "
"--- *except* when it matches the name derived from ``$VIRTUAL_ENV`` (the "
"``prompt = ...`` field in ``<venv>/pyvenv.cfg`` or the venv directory "
"basename). In that case it is treated as an auto-populated value from "
"``activate.xsh`` and wrapped like the other sources, so the prompt reads "
"``(venv) user@host`` instead of ``venvuser@host``. ``$VIRTUAL_ENV_PROMPT`` "
"is overridden by ``$VIRTUAL_ENV_DISABLE_PROMPT``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:523
msgid ""
"When neither variable is set, ``{env_name}`` falls back to the active "
"environment's name --- determined, in order, from the ``prompt = ...`` field "
"in ``<venv>/pyvenv.cfg``, from the venv directory name, or from "
"``$CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV``. The detected name is wrapped in ``{env_prefix}`` and "
"``{env_postfix}`` (``(`` and ``) `` by default)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:529
msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:531
msgid ""
"@ $PROMPT = '{env_name}@ '\n"
"@ source env/bin/activate.xsh\n"
"(env) @ $VIRTUAL_ENV_PROMPT = '~~ACTIVE~~ '\n"
"~~ACTIVE~~ @ $VIRTUAL_ENV_DISABLE_PROMPT = 1\n"
"@ del $VIRTUAL_ENV_PROMPT\n"
"@ del $VIRTUAL_ENV_DISABLE_PROMPT\n"
"(env) @"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:545
msgid "Color theme"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:547
msgid "You can view the available styles by typing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:549
msgid ""
"@ xonfig styles                      # list styles\n"
"@ xonfig colors paraiso-dark         # review how it looks\n"
"@ $XONSH_COLOR_STYLE='paraiso-dark'  # set a new theme"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:556
msgid "Registering custom styles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:558
msgid ""
"If you aren't happy with the styles provided by us (and ``pygments``), you "
"can create and register custom styles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:560
msgid "To do so, add something similar to your :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:562
msgid ""
"from xonsh.tools import register_custom_style\n"
"mystyle = {\n"
"    \"Literal.String.Single\": \"#ff88aa\",\n"
"    \"Literal.String.Double\": \"#ff4488\",\n"
"    \"RED\": \"#008800\",\n"
"}\n"
"register_custom_style(\"mystyle\", mystyle, base=\"monokai\")\n"
"$XONSH_COLOR_STYLE = \"mystyle\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:573
msgid ""
"You can check ``xonfig colors`` for the token names. The ``base`` style will "
"be used as a fallback for styles you don't set - pick one from ``xonfig "
"styles`` (``default`` is used if omitted)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:577
msgid "OSC 7 — Working directory reporting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../prompt.rst:579
msgid ""
"Xonsh automatically emits `OSC 7 <https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/terminal-wg/"
"specifications/-/merge_requests/7>`_ escape sequences on every directory "
"change and at shell startup. This is an invisible signal that tells the "
"terminal emulator what the current working directory is."
msgstr ""

